<description>
Complete the separated array of one-hole tiles.

Input structure
A square grid has one background color and one foreground color. Its side
length is n*n+n-1, partitioning it into an n by n array of n by n tiles
separated by single background rows and columns. Some tiles are already
present; each present tile is filled with foreground except for one
background pixel at the local coordinate matching the tile's array position.

Rule
Keep the separator rows and columns background. For every tile position
(tile row, tile column), fill that n by n tile with the foreground color and
leave only the local pixel at (tile row, tile column) as background.

Key insight
The hole inside each tile is a coordinate label for that tile's position in
the larger tile array, so the sparse input samples define the complete
position-coded pattern.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "partial n by n array of n by n foreground tiles separated by one background row and column; each visible tile has one background pixel at the local coordinate matching its array coordinate",
  "dst": "empty tile slots in the same separated array",
  "transform": "complete every tile as foreground except for its coordinate-label background pixel, and keep separator rows and columns background"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: square, side = n*n + n - 1, where n in 2-5; observed n in {2,3,4}
  colors: exactly 2 — one bg (most common, always 0 [observed]), one fg
  structure: |
    Grid is an n x n array of n x n tiles separated by single bg rows/columns.
    Separator rows/cols are fully bg. Each tile is either:
      - present: filled with fg except one bg pixel at local coord (tile_row, tile_col)
      - absent: entirely bg
  tiles:
    total: n*n (4-25)
    present_count: >=1 and < n*n (some absent so task is non-trivial)
    fill_ratio: 0.4-0.6 [observed]
    placement: no required pattern; can leave entire rows/cols of tiles empty [observed]
  output: all n*n tiles become present; separators stay bg

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (output is fully determined by n and colors)
  steps:
    1. Pick n in 2-5, pick bg=0, pick fg != bg
    2. Build output grid (n*n+n-1 square): fill every tile with fg, place bg hole at (tile_r, tile_c) in each tile, keep separator rows/cols as bg
    3. Choose a random subset of tiles to keep present; ensure >=1 present and >=1 absent; target fill ratio ~0.4-0.6
    4. Build input by copying output then zeroing out absent tiles to all bg
```
</generation_spec>
